{"name":"Balsam Fir","hex":"#909e91","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"balsam fir","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A North American species of fir tree (Abies balsamea)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/balsam_fir"}],"entries":[{"title":"Abies balsamea","blocks":[{"text":"Abies balsamea or balsam fir is a North American fir, native to most of eastern and central Canada and the northeastern United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Abies_balsamea.jpg/330px-Abies_balsamea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abies_balsamea"},{"title":"Balsam","blocks":[{"text":"Balsam is the resinous exudate which forms on certain kinds of trees and shrubs, particularly species belonging to genera such as Myroxylon, Abies, Copaifera, Styrax, and Populus. They may occur naturally as resinous exudates following injury to bark or wood tissues. Balsam owes its name to the biblical Balm of Gilead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Balsamum_tolutanum1.JPG/330px-Balsamum_tolutanum1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balsam"},{"title":"Fir","blocks":[{"text":"Firs are coniferous trees belonging to the genus Abies in the family Pinaceae. There are approximately 48–65 extant species, found on mountains throughout much of North and Central America, Eurasia, and North Africa. The genus is most closely related to Keteleeria, a small genus confined to eastern Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Abies_koreana_%28szyszki%29.JPG/330px-Abies_koreana_%28szyszki%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fir"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Carter_Dome_seen_from_Mt_Hight.JPG/5